<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tréguier</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tréguier is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotes-darmor">Côtes-d'Armor</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern Brittany, where the Jaudy and Guindy rivers meet. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1790, and its Gothic cathedral holds the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-of-kermartin">Saint Yves</a>, who was born nearby. The first Breton dictionary, the Catholicon, was printed there in 1499, and it is the birthplace of the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treguier.jpg" alt="Tréguier" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_Cotes_d_Armor_Treguier_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tréguier, Landreger in Breton, is a port town and the capital of the province of Trégor. It lies 36 miles north-west of Saint-Brieuc by road. The port is about 5 and a half miles from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, at the confluence of two streams that form the Tréguier River. Its inhabitants are called trécorrois in French. In the past saw-milling, boat-building and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a>-stripping were carried on there, together with trade in cereals, cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, and the port had fishing, coasting and a small foreign trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tréguier, called Trecorum, dates from the sixth century and grew up round a monastery founded by Saint Tudwal, who died about 564. In the 9th century it became the seat of a bishopric, which was suppressed on 12 July 1790 under a decree of 14 November 1789. Count Stephen of Tréguier was the second Earl of Richmond, inheriting the peerage that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> had created for his second cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-rufus">Alan Rufus</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> established a naval air station there on 1 November 1918 to operate seaplanes during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and it closed shortly after the Armistice of Compiègne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cathedral, dating from the 14th and 15th centuries, has three towers over the transept, one of them with a fine spire. It holds the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-of-kermartin">Ivo of Kermartin</a> (Saint Yves), who died in 1303, a canon of the cathedral and patron saint of lawyers, to whom its building was largely due. South of the church is a cloister of 1468. The Pardon of Saint Yves draws lawyers and judges from many countries, and the Tro Breizh pilgrimage links Tréguier with the towns of the other founding saints of Brittany.</p><p>A statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>, born in the town in 1823, stands near the cathedral. Renan was a prominent sceptic, and when the statue was unveiled in 1903 with a figure of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, it provoked widespread protests from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. The town has a museum in his birthplace. Others born there include the critic Ernest Hello and the Dominican Hervaeus Natalis, who was Master General of the order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tréguier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholicon, a Breton–French–Latin dictionary compiled in 1464 and printed at <b>Tréguier</b> in 1499, is the first Breton dictionary and one of the earliest of French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The printer Jehan Calvez of <b>Tréguier</b> published the first edition of the Catholicon, a dictionary of Breton, French and Latin compiled in 1464 by Jehan Lagadeuc.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-5">5 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
